@page "/test/cookie-secure/check"
@model OperaLegacyLab.Web.Pages.Test.CookieSecureCheckModel
<h1>Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookie test - result</h1>
<p>The previous page tried to set a cookie with three attributes at once:
<tt>HttpOnly</tt>, <tt>Secure</tt>, and <tt>SameSite=Strict</tt> - all newer
than Opera 6.x itself (<tt>SameSite</tt> especially; it didn't exist as a
concept until 2016). The basic check below only proves the cookie's value
survived one round trip - see the two follow-up checks further down for
whether Secure/HttpOnly are actually being enforced, not just tolerated.</p>

@if (!Model.RequestWasHttps)
{
    <p><b>Note:</b> this request did not arrive over https.</p>
}
@if (Model.Present)
{
    <p><b>Cookie works.</b> The server set this cookie (with HttpOnly, Secure,
    and SameSite=Strict all set) on the previous request, and your browser
    sent it straight back on this one.</p>
}
else
{
    <p><b>Cookie did NOT come back.</b> Either this browser doesn't support
    one or more of HttpOnly/Secure/SameSite well enough to still accept and
    store the cookie, or this simply wasn't reached over a secure connection.</p>
}
<p>Raw Cookie header this browser sent just now:<br>
<tt>@Model.RawCookieHeader</tt></p>

<h2>Is Secure actually enforced?</h2>
<p>The check above only shows the cookie survived an https round trip - that
alone doesn't prove anything about Secure, since it would look identical
whether or not this browser enforces it. The real test: does the SAME
cookie also come back over a plain http connection to this same address?
If it does, Secure isn't being enforced; if it's genuinely missing there,
it is.</p>
@if (Model.Lab.SecureCookieSeenOverPlainHttp.HasValue)
{
    if (Model.Lab.SecureCookieSeenOverPlainHttp.Value)
    {
        <p><b>Secure is NOT enforced</b> - the cookie came back even over plain http.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p><b>Secure IS enforced</b> - the cookie was correctly withheld over plain http.</p>
    }
}
else if (Model.RequestWasHttps)
{
    <p><a href="@Model.PlainHttpCheckUrl">Click here to try loading this same check page over plain http</a>
    to find out. (This link only works at all if your ngrok tunnel happens to expose this same address
    over plain http as well as https - on the real device, reached only through ngrok's https tunnel,
    it may simply fail to load at all. That failure is itself a real, useful answer: it just means this
    particular check can't be run from here, not that Secure is broken.)</p>
}
else
{
    <p>(Not tested yet - not applicable from a plain-http visit; start from the https version of this test.)</p>
}

<h2>Is HttpOnly actually enforced?</h2>
<p>HttpOnly's entire point is hiding a cookie from JavaScript's own
<tt>document.cookie</tt> - the only way to check it is to actually ask
JavaScript, which the script below does automatically (no click needed,
using the exact same document.write technique as the JavaScript capability
tests elsewhere in this lab).</p>
@if (Model.Lab.HttpOnlyVisibleToJs.HasValue)
{
    if (Model.Lab.HttpOnlyVisibleToJs.Value)
    {
        <p><b>HttpOnly is NOT enforced</b> - JavaScript's document.cookie could see this cookie.</p>
    }
    else if (Model.Present)
    {
        <p><b>HttpOnly IS enforced</b> - the cookie arrived in the raw request header above, but
        JavaScript's document.cookie could NOT see it.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>JavaScript ran and reported the cookie wasn't visible to it - but the cookie didn't arrive at
        all on this request either (see above), so this doesn't actually demonstrate HttpOnly working,
        just that there was nothing there either way.</p>
    }
}
else
{
    <p>(Not yet determined - either JavaScript hasn't run on this page yet, or this browser doesn't run
    JavaScript at all, in which case this specific check can never be completed here.)</p>
}
@if (!Model.HttpOnlyAlreadyProbed)
{
    <script type="text/javascript">
    var cookieVisibleToJs = (document.cookie.indexOf('cookietest_secure') >= 0);
    document.write('<form name="httpOnlyProbe" method="post" action="/test/cookie-secure/check?lab=@Model.Lab.Code">');
    document.write('<input type="hidden" name="httpOnlyVisible" value="' + (cookieVisibleToJs ? '1' : '0') + '">');
    document.write('</form>');
    document.httpOnlyProbe.submit();
    </script>
}

<h2>What about SameSite=Strict?</h2>
<p>Deliberately not tested by this lab. <tt>SameSite</tt> only ever matters
for a request that originates from a genuinely different site than this
cookie's own origin - every request this lab can make or ask you to make is
same-origin navigation, so there is no way to give <tt>SameSite=Strict</tt>
a chance to actually block anything from here. Confirming it for real would
need a link hosted on a separate, different domain, which this single-origin
lab doesn't have.</p>
